---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

/*
  IMAGES : deposer le dossier `living-system/` dans /public/
  - photos     : credit EXIF/XMP Madeleine Photographe / studiomadeleine.fr
  - pages spec : credit EXIF/XMP Lea Giezek (c'est ton document)
*/
---
<Base title="Living System - Léa Giezek"
  current="system-event">

<!-- CARD 1 : banner + intro -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="banner">
      <a class="banner__home" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 12H5"/><path d="m11 18-6-6 6-6"/></svg>Back to home</a>
      <div class="banner__inner">
        <p class="banner__standards">The same thinking,<br>off the <span class="banner__culture">screen.</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="p-intro" id="intro">
      <h1 class="p-eyebrow">Living System</h1>
      <p class="p-intro__lead">Over more than a year, I designed and orchestrated a live event from end to end — one identity, one experience, held together across every surface and every supplier. No repository. No deploy pipeline. Just the discipline of making a hundred moving parts feel like one thing.</p>
      <p class="p-intro__lead">It's the proof I keep coming back to: <b>my way of working doesn't depend on the medium.</b> Systems thinking, brand coherence, experience design — they hold whether the output ships to production or lives in a garden for one day.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- THESIS -->
  <section class="ls-thesis ls-reveal" aria-label="The system, in the world">
    <figure class="ls-fig ls-thesis__fig">
      <img src="/living-system/staging-arch.webp" width="1400" height="2100" alt="Ceremony arch hung with shapes from the event's visual identity, framed by floral pedestals and two seats" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
    </figure>
    <div class="ls-thesis__body">
      <p class="ls-pull">A living system isn't one you finish. It's one that takes its real shape <em>in the hands of the people who use it</em> — here, off the screen, exactly as it does on it.</p>
      <p class="ls-note">Not product. Not digital. Not communication. A living one — and it held together just the same.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 IDENTITY -->
  <section class="ls-chapter ls-reveal" aria-labelledby="ch-identity">
    <div class="ls-head">
      <p class="ls-eyebrow"><span>01 — Identity</span></p>
      <h2 class="ls-title" id="ch-identity">One identity, everywhere.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ls-origin">
      <div class="ls-origin__objects">
        <figure class="ls-fig ls-origin__fig">
          <img src="/living-system/identity-source-plate.webp" width="1400" height="1008" alt="Hand-painted scallop-edged Popolo plate laid on a wooden table" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">Popolo plate — Maison Options</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="ls-fig ls-origin__fig">
          <img src="/living-system/identity-source-chair.webp" width="1400" height="820" alt="Two rounded sculptural armchairs with striped cushions, set on a rug beneath the ceremony arch" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">Roly Poly armchair — Agence Parade</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-origin__body">
        <p class="ls-origin__label">The whole thing started with two objects.</p>
        <p class="ls-origin__words"><span>Shapes</span><span>Type</span><span>Colors</span><span class="is-key">Conviviality</span><span class="is-key">Playfulness</span></p>
        <p class="ls-body">Not a moodboard and not a colour study — two rented pieces we could actually put in the space. The <b>Popolo</b> plate: scalloped rim, hand-painted florals, cobalt and ochre. The <b>Roly Poly</b> armchair: fat legs, round shoulders, no straight line anywhere. One gave the pattern language. The other gave the posture.</p>
        <p class="ls-body">Five words came out of them, written down before anything was drawn — then <b>systematised end to end across the whole experience</b>, from the first envelope to the last jar label.</p>
        <p class="ls-body">The scallop became the die-cut on the stationery and the discs hung from the arch. The chair's soft geometry became the arched panels on the signage. The palette came straight off the glaze. And the last two — <b>conviviality and playfulness</b>, the ones that aren't visual properties at all — are the reason the day ended up with an escape game in it.</p>
        <p class="ls-note">Starting from a constraint you don't own, then making every other decision agree with it. That's most of the job on the platform too.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ls-head ls-head--after">
      <p class="ls-body">From there, a full visual identity, declined across every physical surface the day would touch: a monogram, a palette, signage, stationery, room tags, table names, favours, packaging. Dozens of touchpoints, one language — the same coherence I hold across 45+ digital products, applied to paper, fabric, metal and wood.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ls-scatter" aria-label="Surfaces carrying the identity">
      <figure class="ls-shot ls-shot--p" style="--x:0%;--y:44px;--rot:-4deg">
        <img src="/living-system/identity-welcome-sign.webp" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Welcome sign on an easel showing the event monogram, beside stacked metal trunks" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>Signage</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="ls-shot ls-shot--p" style="--x:20%;--y:0px;--rot:2.5deg">
        <img src="/living-system/identity-invitation-suite.webp" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Invitation suite of layered printed cards and coloured envelopes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>Stationery</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="ls-shot ls-shot--p" style="--x:41%;--y:88px;--rot:-2deg">
        <img src="/living-system/identity-monogram-arch.webp" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Monogram disc and a striped disc hanging from the ceremony arch above a floral pedestal" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>Monogram</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="ls-shot ls-shot--p" style="--x:62%;--y:12px;--rot:4deg">
        <img src="/living-system/identity-cards.webp" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Wall rack holding message cards in the identity's colours and patterns" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>Message cards</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="ls-shot ls-shot--p" style="--x:81%;--y:58px;--rot:-3deg">
        <img src="/living-system/identity-message-desk.webp" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Writing desk set up for guests with coloured pens and a framed instruction card" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>Writing desk</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="ls-claimrow">
      <p class="ls-claim">Nothing was left to chance, and nothing drifted.<br><b>One identity, fifty-plus surfaces, zero inconsistency.</b></p>
      <div class="ls-claimrow__figs">
        <figure class="ls-fig ls-fig--soft">
          <img src="/living-system/identity-sign-manifesto.webp" width="1100" height="901" alt="Printed sign reading Heart Open, Phone Down, set in the identity's typography" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">A board in the garden</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="ls-fig ls-fig--soft ls-claimrow__label">
          <img src="/living-system/print-jar-label.webp" width="1200" height="676" alt="Print artwork for the jar label: striped band carrying the monogram, shown with its registration marks" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">A jar label — 62 × 25 mm</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ls-signage">
      <div class="ls-signage__text">
        <p class="ls-sub">The test was the boring surfaces.</p>
        <p class="ls-body">Anyone can be charming on an invitation. The proof is on the signage nobody is meant to enjoy — where to leave your glass, what the bar does after midnight. Same display face, same stripes, same jokes.</p>
        <p class="ls-body">That's where <b>conviviality and playfulness</b> stopped being words on a moodboard and became a rule I had to keep, on the two hundredth decision as much as the first.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-signage__figs">
        <figure class="ls-fig ls-fig--flat">
          <img src="/living-system/print-night-bar.webp" width="1200" height="1571" alt="Print artwork for the night bar sign, striped and set in the identity's display type" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">Night bar — 300 × 400 mm</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="ls-fig ls-fig--flat">
          <img src="/living-system/print-glass-rules.webp" width="1100" height="1545" alt="Print artwork for the small card explaining what to do with your glass through the evening" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">Glass rules — 100 × 150 mm</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="ba" style="--ba-ratio:3/4">
      <div class="ba__side ba__side--spec">
        <p class="ba__label">Artwork</p>
        <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/print-invitation-date-recto.webp" width="1300" height="1784" alt="Print-ready artwork for the invitation card, shown with its crop marks and bleed" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
      </div>
      <div class="ba__side ba__side--built">
        <p class="ba__label">Printed</p>
        <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/identity-invitation-open.webp" width="1600" height="1067" alt="The finished invitation opened out on a wooden table, its die-cut shapes and colour blocks fanned out" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="ba__cap">Type outlined, colours flattened, bleed set, sent to <a href="https://www.imprimerie-paquereau.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Les Ateliers Paquereau</a>. Every card in the suite went through the same file.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 STAGING -->
  <section class="ls-chapter ls-reveal" aria-labelledby="ch-staging">
    <div class="ls-split">
      <div class="ls-split__text">
        <p class="ls-eyebrow"><span>02 — Staging</span></p>
        <h2 class="ls-title" id="ch-staging">Space, staged.</h2>
        <p class="ls-body">An implantation plan for the day, then the scenography that brought it to life: the arch, the seating, the floral pedestals, the palette carried into every corner of the space.</p>
        <p class="ls-body">The system on paper, and the system in the world — <b>the same jump from specification to production I run on the platform</b>, made physical.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-split__media">
        <figure class="ls-fig">
          <img src="/living-system/staging-hall.webp" width="1800" height="1200" alt="Reception hall under exposed beams and string lights, long tables laid for a hundred guests" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">The room, at scale</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="ls-fig">
          <img src="/living-system/staging-table.webp" width="1600" height="1067" alt="Long table with patterned plates, gold cutlery and single-stem vases running down the centre" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">Tablescape</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="ls-pair">
          <figure class="ls-fig">
            <img src="/living-system/staging-pedestal.webp" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Floral pedestal in the event's palette next to a printed menu panel" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption class="ls-cap">Pedestal</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="ls-fig">
            <img src="/living-system/staging-flowers.webp" width="1400" height="933" alt="Close view of the flower palette: white daisies, blue cornflowers and pink blooms" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption class="ls-cap">Palette</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="ba" style="--ba-ratio:4/5">
      <div class="ba__side ba__side--spec">
        <p class="ba__label">Specified</p>
        <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/spec-zone-seating.webp" width="1400" height="1615" alt="Page from the production guide: mockup of the seating panels with the material checklist" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
      </div>
      <div class="ba__side ba__side--built">
        <p class="ba__label">Built</p>
        <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/staging-entrance-evening.webp" width="1200" height="1800" alt="The seating panels installed at the venue doorway at dusk, flanked by floral columns" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="ba__cap">Drawn in Illustrator, briefed to four suppliers, standing at the door twelve months later.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 THE GAME -->
  <section class="ls-chapter ls-reveal" aria-labelledby="ch-game">
    <div class="ls-head">
      <p class="ls-eyebrow"><span>03 — The centrepiece</span></p>
      <h2 class="ls-title" id="ch-game">A game, by design.</h2>
      <p class="ls-body">An escape game, designed from zero — rules, clues, progression, reward. A locked trunk, a sequence to crack, a payoff at the end. Experience design in its purest form: a system of constraints and steps that has to <em>feel</em> effortless while being tightly engineered underneath.</p>
      <p class="ls-body"><b>Six riddles, six different mechanics for six essential clues to unlock one final chest, all together.</b> Each had to be learnable in thirty seconds, solvable by people holding a drink, and unmistakably part of the same day as the invitation and the table.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ls-artefacts">
      <div class="ls-artefacts__stack">
        <figure class="ls-fig ls-fig--soft ls-artefacts__brief">
          <img src="/living-system/print-game-brief.webp" width="1500" height="1067" alt="Print artwork for the escape game briefing card, set in the identity's display type with scattered paw prints" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">The pitch — the card that starts the whole thing</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="ls-fig ls-fig--soft ls-artefacts__label">
          <img src="/living-system/print-game-poster.webp" width="1200" height="1571" alt="Print artwork for the poster announcing the escape game to guests" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap">The announcement — 300 × 400 mm</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <figure class="ls-fig ls-fig--soft ls-artefacts__board">
        <img src="/living-system/print-game-cats.webp" width="1500" height="2100" alt="Print artwork for the first riddle: a nine by nine colour-region grid with its single rule set above it" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption class="ls-cap">Riddle 01 — a region-constraint logic grid, reskinned</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="ls-artefacts__note">One rule, stated in a sentence: one paw per colour, per row, per column. The hard part of game design isn't the riddle — it's making the riddle explainable in a line, and making the line sound like the rest of the party.</p>

    <ol class="ls-steps">
      <li class="ls-step">
        <figure class="ls-fig">
          <img src="/living-system/game-locked.webp" width="1600" height="1067" alt="Hands working a combination padlock on a metal trunk, playing cards and jars set out on the lid" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
        <p class="ls-step__t"><span class="ls-step__n">01</span>The constraint</p>
        <p class="ls-step__b">A locked trunk, a combination, and clues scattered across the day.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="ls-step">
        <figure class="ls-fig">
          <img src="/living-system/game-opened.webp" width="1200" height="1800" alt="Opened trunk revealing a framed congratulations card above rows of jars" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
        <p class="ls-step__t"><span class="ls-step__n">02</span>The crack</p>
        <p class="ls-step__b">Progression built so the last step feels earned, not handed over.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="ls-step">
        <figure class="ls-fig">
          <img src="/living-system/game-reward.webp" width="1600" height="1067" alt="Rows of jars packed in the trunk, each labelled with the event monogram on striped paper" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
        <p class="ls-step__t"><span class="ls-step__n">03</span>The payoff</p>
        <p class="ls-step__b">The reward carries the identity too. The system runs to the last object.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="ls-body ls-body--wide">
      <p>But here's what I love most about it: <b>it only became itself once people played it.</b> I designed the frame — tight, deliberate, engineered underneath — and the experience took its real shape in their hands, evolving as they moved through it, in ways no plan could fully script.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="ls-sub">Shipped empty. Finished by the people who played it.</p>

    <figure class="ba" style="--ba-ratio:4/5">
      <div class="ba__side ba__side--spec">
        <p class="ba__label">Specified</p>
        <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/spec-zone-murdle.webp" width="1400" height="1515" alt="Production guide page for the deduction game: empty grid on an easel, material checklist and set-up notes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
      </div>
      <div class="ba__side ba__side--built">
        <p class="ba__label">Played</p>
        <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/game-murdle.webp" width="1100" height="1650" alt="The same deduction board after play, its grid filled with pink and green wooden tokens" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="ba__cap">The deduction grid, before anyone touched it and after a hundred people did.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="ba" style="--ba-ratio:4/5">
      <div class="ba__side ba__side--spec">
        <p class="ba__label">Specified</p>
        <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/spec-zone-wordsearch.webp" width="1400" height="1445" alt="Production guide page for the word-grid game: blank poster on an easel with its material checklist" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
      </div>
      <div class="ba__side ba__side--built">
        <p class="ba__label">Played</p>
        <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/game-wordsearch.webp" width="1100" height="733" alt="The word grid mid-game, crossed through with chalk lines as players work it out together" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="ba__cap">I drew the grid. The marks on it aren't mine — and that was always the point.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="ls-body ls-body--wide">
      <p>That's the whole truth about the systems I build: <b>you design them for the people who'll use them, not for yourself — and they come alive the moment you let go.</b> An escape game, a design system, a platform: same principle.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="ls-claim ls-claim--big">The frame is mine.<br>The life is theirs.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 COORDINATION -->
  <section class="ls-chapter ls-reveal" aria-labelledby="ch-coordination">
    <div class="card ls-dark">
      <div class="ls-dark__inner">
        <p class="ls-eyebrow ls-eyebrow--light"><span>04 — Coordination</span></p>
        <h2 class="ls-title" id="ch-coordination">The part nobody sees.</h2>
        <div class="ls-body ls-body--light">
          <p>Behind the identity and the staging: coordination. Florist, caterer, photographer, venue, rentals — a full ecosystem of suppliers, each with their own constraints, all pulling toward one coherent experience.</p>
          <p>I wrote it all down. Zone by zone: what goes where, what it's made of, what it's for. <b>A production guide precise enough to be executed without me in the room</b> — then I handed the day itself to <a href="https://hera-et-harmonia.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hera &amp; Harmonia</a>, who ran it from the brief.</p>
        </div>

        <figure class="ba ba--dark" style="--ba-ratio:5/4">
          <div class="ba__side ba__side--spec">
            <p class="ba__label">Briefed</p>
            <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/spec-zone-bar.webp" width="1500" height="713" alt="Production guide page for the cocktail area: layout reference, furniture list and set-up notes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          </div>
          <div class="ba__side ba__side--built">
            <p class="ba__label">Run without me</p>
            <div class="ba__frame"><img src="/living-system/coordination-bar.webp" width="1200" height="1800" alt="A bartender building a drink at the cocktail station during the event" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="ba__cap">One page per zone, one owner per page. That's the whole method.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="ls-body ls-body--light">
          <p>Because the point was never to hold every thread myself. It was to build something clear enough that someone else could carry it, and to trust them to. On the day, the system held, and I got to be <em>in</em> the experience instead of running it.</p>
        </div>

        <figure class="ls-fig ls-fig--wide">
          <img src="/living-system/coordination-embrace.webp" width="1600" height="1067" alt="Guests embracing at the event, the designer among them rather than running the day" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption class="ls-cap ls-cap--light">Inside it, not behind it.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p class="ls-note ls-note--light">The same move as everything else I build: design the system, brief the owners, step back. It works in a repo. It works in a garden.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CLOSING -->
  <section class="ls-closing ls-reveal">
    <p class="ls-closing__t">I don't just repeat a system.<br>I <em>version</em> it.</p>
    <p class="ls-closing__b">This wasn't a one-off. It was the third edition — each one bigger than the last: a full identity every time, an escape game every time, the level raised at every turn. The pattern doesn't stop at the screen — and it doesn't stop at work either.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- CREDITS -->
  <section class="ls-credits ls-reveal" aria-labelledby="ch-credits">
    <h2 class="ls-credits__t" id="ch-credits">Credits</h2>
    <p class="ls-credits__intro">Identity, game design, scenography, print artwork and the production guide are mine. Everything else was built by people far better than me at their own craft.</p>
    <dl class="ls-credits__list">
      <div class="ls-credits__row">
        <dt>Photography</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://studiomadeleine.fr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Madeleine Photographe&nbsp;©</a></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-credits__row">
        <dt>Day-of coordination</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://hera-et-harmonia.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hera &amp; Harmonia</a></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-credits__row">
        <dt>Venue</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://www.legrandbreuil.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Le Grand Breuil</a></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-credits__row">
        <dt>Furniture</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://agence-parade.fr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agence Parade</a></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-credits__row">
        <dt>Print</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://www.imprimerie-paquereau.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Les Ateliers Paquereau</a></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-credits__row">
        <dt>Floral design</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://lesnanas-studiofloral.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Les Nanas</a></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-credits__row">
        <dt>Tableware &amp; linen</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://www.options.fr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Maison Options</a></dd>
      </div>
      <div class="ls-credits__row">
        <dt>Cocktail bar</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://sobarsodrink.squarespace.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Le Horse Bar</a></dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="ls-credits__note">All photographs on this page &copy; Madeleine Photographe. Production documents and print artwork &copy; Léa Giezek.</p>
  </section>

</Base>

<style is:global>
  .banner__standards{font-family:var(--display);font-weight:400;font-size:clamp(28px,3.6vw,48px);line-height:1.04;letter-spacing:-.01em}
  .p-intro__lead + .p-intro__lead{margin-top:1em}

  /* --- shared -------------------------------------------------------- */
  .ls-fig{margin:0;overflow:hidden;border-radius:var(--r-lg);background:var(--tile-red);line-height:0}
  .ls-fig img{display:block;width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover}
  .ls-fig--soft{background:var(--card)}
  .ls-fig--wide{margin-top:clamp(24px,3.4vw,40px);aspect-ratio:3/2}
  [data-theme="contrast"] .ls-fig{border:2px solid var(--line)}
  .ls-cap{line-height:1.6;margin-top:10px;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-muted)}
  .ls-cap--light{color:rgba(255,255,255,.68)}

